{"id":"q11","run":1,"role":"calibration","category":"puzzle","question":"If a hen and a half lays an egg and a half in a day and a half, do 3 hens lay 3 eggs in 3 days?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":null,"claims":[{"text":"It is tempting to assume eggs always match hens because the egg count equals the hen count in each setup.","confidence":0.3,"lean":1,"theta":0.4712,"phi":1.5943},{"text":"The time values appear to scale in step with the number of hens.","confidence":0.3,"lean":1,"theta":0.4712,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Egg output depends on both the number of hens and the number of days.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.0761},{"text":"Each hen lays 1 egg in 1.5 days, i.e. 2/3 egg per day.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.069},{"text":"One hen lays 2 eggs in 3 days.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.0708},{"text":"Three hens lay 6 eggs in 3 days, not 3.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":3.0675},{"text":"Doubling both hens and days multiplies output by 4, giving 6 eggs from the original 1.5.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.069},{"text":"The assumption that eggs match hens is a misleading pattern that does not hold.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":2.6439},{"text":"The answer to the question is NO.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":2.9738}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":3,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":4,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":5,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":6,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":8,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":4,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":5,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":6,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":8,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":4,"j":5,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":4,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":4,"j